{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VqGYqx3RjQpf4YbSXjM96F","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WjqTeLLLBSPS4fQbQ6HiJR","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus is **methods and experiments**. You are doing a blind review — evaluate the paper purely on its merits.\n\n## Venue Calibration (EMNLP 2026 via ARR)\n- 5 = Award (top 2.5%), 4 = Main conference, 3 = Findings, 2 = Resubmit, 1 = Do not resubmit\n- Dimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5)\n- Acceptance rate ~21% for Main\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"All Ambiguity Is Nocuous: A Causal Study of Policy Specification Ambiguity and Agent Safety Violations\"\n\nThe paper studies how linguistic ambiguity in natural-language policy specifications causes safety violations in LLM agents. Using a matched-pair design, 300 clause pairs across 6 ambiguity types, 4 models (GPT-5.4, GPT-4.1, Qwen3-32B-AWQ, Qwen3-8B), and 2,400 agentic episodes on τ²-bench, they establish 5 claims:\n- C1: Ambiguous clauses cause +33.6 pp increase in violation rate (all 6 types p < 3e-7)\n- C2: Ambiguity type does not differentially predict violation severity (χ²(5)=5.20, p=0.39, V=0.066) — binary detection suffices\n- C3: Visible conflicts (CP) yield fewer violations than invisible ambiguity (scopal): Δ=-11pp, p=0.015\n- C4: Model capability predicts type sensitivity — stronger models show wider type-specific ranges (22pp vs 4pp), interaction p=0.018\n- C5: Error analysis identifies 8 failure modes with strong type-clustering\n\nKey tables: Table 1 (binary effect), Table 2 (type uniformity), Table 3 (visibility), Table 4 (4×6 violation matrix), Table 5 (failure mode taxonomy), Table 6 (visibility mechanism evidence), Table 7 (classifier PoC).\n\nAppendices cover: taxonomy cross-walk (A), clause generation (B), reliability ICC=0.82 (C), realism validation with KS tests all non-significant (D).\n\nPaper is 8 pages main text + references + appendices. Anonymous submission.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full experimental plan included these experiments:\n1. full_study_clause_generation: 300 clauses (50/type × 6 types), Chantree-style quality audit, 94% pass rate\n2. full_study_api: 1,200 episodes with GPT-5.4 and GPT-4.1\n3. full_study_local: 1,200 episodes with Qwen3-32B-AWQ and Qwen3-8B via vLLM\n4. full_study_analysis: Statistical analysis — binary effect, omnibus χ², visibility, interaction\n5. error_analysis: 300 violation traces, 8 failure modes, inter-annotator κ=0.78\n6. reliability_test: 30 clauses × 3 replicates × 4 models = 720 episodes, ICC=0.82\n7. realism_validation: KS tests comparing synthetic vs DRIP-R natural ambiguities\n8. classifier_poc: DeBERTa-v3-large binary classifier, 88% accuracy, AUC 0.93\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, specific)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, specific, with severity: major/minor)\n4. **Methodological Scrutiny** — evaluate:\n   - Matched-pair design validity and confounds\n   - Statistical analysis: appropriate tests? Multiple comparison corrections? TOST equivalence logic?\n   - Cross-judge evaluation (κ=0.91): is this sufficient? Any circularity risk with LLM judge?\n   - Reliability study: ICC interpretation, sample size for reliability\n   - Realism validation: KS tests + discriminator — sufficient for ecological validity?\n   - Classifier PoC: role in paper, appropriate scope?\n5. **Experimental Coverage** — compare what the paper reports vs. the experiment list above. Are key results missing or underreported?\n6. **Questions for Authors** (3-5 specific questions)\n7. **Scores**:\n   - Soundness (1-5)\n   - Excitement (1-5)\n   - Reproducibility (1-5)\n   - Overall Assessment (1-5, half-point scale per EMNLP 2026)\n   - Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Calibrate to EMNLP acceptance rate (~21% main, plus Findings). A paper scoring 4.0 is borderline Main acceptance."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":35650,"cache_read_input_tokens":23505,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":35650},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ed42e909-7586-4fce-8151-53367c735e4f","uuid":"d1cb852a-517c-4b7c-a78a-320a12a8223f"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"ae3f753dfa0669638","tool_use_id":"toolu_01WjqTeLLLBSPS4fQbQ6HiJR","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus is **methods and experiments**. You are doing a blind review — evaluate the paper purely on its merits.\n\n## Venue Calibration (EMNLP 2026 via ARR)\n- 5 = Award (top 2.5%), 4 = Main conference, 3 = Findings, 2 = Resubmit, 1 = Do not resubmit\n- Dimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5)\n- Acceptance rate ~21% for Main\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"All Ambiguity Is Nocuous: A Causal Study of Policy Specification Ambiguity and Agent Safety Violations\"\n\nThe paper studies how linguistic ambiguity in natural-language policy specifications causes safety violations in LLM agents. Using a matched-pair design, 300 clause pairs across 6 ambiguity types, 4 models (GPT-5.4, GPT-4.1, Qwen3-32B-AWQ, Qwen3-8B), and 2,400 agentic episodes on τ²-bench, they establish 5 claims:\n- C1: Ambiguous clauses cause +33.6 pp increase in violation rate (all 6 types p < 3e-7)\n- C2: Ambiguity type does not differentially predict violation severity (χ²(5)=5.20, p=0.39, V=0.066) — binary detection suffices\n- C3: Visible conflicts (CP) yield fewer violations than invisible ambiguity (scopal): Δ=-11pp, p=0.015\n- C4: Model capability predicts type sensitivity — stronger models show wider type-specific ranges (22pp vs 4pp), interaction p=0.018\n- C5: Error analysis identifies 8 failure modes with strong type-clustering\n\nKey tables: Table 1 (binary effect), Table 2 (type uniformity), Table 3 (visibility), Table 4 (4×6 violation matrix), Table 5 (failure mode taxonomy), Table 6 (visibility mechanism evidence), Table 7 (classifier PoC).\n\nAppendices cover: taxonomy cross-walk (A), clause generation (B), reliability ICC=0.82 (C), realism validation with KS tests all non-significant (D).\n\nPaper is 8 pages main text + references + appendices. Anonymous submission.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full experimental plan included these experiments:\n1. full_study_clause_generation: 300 clauses (50/type × 6 types), Chantree-style quality audit, 94% pass rate\n2. full_study_api: 1,200 episodes with GPT-5.4 and GPT-4.1\n3. full_study_local: 1,200 episodes with Qwen3-32B-AWQ and Qwen3-8B via vLLM\n4. full_study_analysis: Statistical analysis — binary effect, omnibus χ², visibility, interaction\n5. error_analysis: 300 violation traces, 8 failure modes, inter-annotator κ=0.78\n6. reliability_test: 30 clauses × 3 replicates × 4 models = 720 episodes, ICC=0.82\n7. realism_validation: KS tests comparing synthetic vs DRIP-R natural ambiguities\n8. classifier_poc: DeBERTa-v3-large binary classifier, 88% accuracy, AUC 0.93\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, specific)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, specific, with severity: major/minor)\n4. **Methodological Scrutiny** — evaluate:\n   - Matched-pair design validity and confounds\n   - Statistical analysis: appropriate tests? Multiple comparison corrections? TOST equivalence logic?\n   - Cross-judge evaluation (κ=0.91): is this sufficient? Any circularity risk with LLM judge?\n   - Reliability study: ICC interpretation, sample size for reliability\n   - Realism validation: KS tests + discriminator — sufficient for ecological validity?\n   - Classifier PoC: role in paper, appropriate scope?\n5. **Experimental Coverage** — compare what the paper reports vs. the experiment list above. Are key results missing or underreported?\n6. **Questions for Authors** (3-5 specific questions)\n7. **Scores**:\n   - Soundness (1-5)\n   - Excitement (1-5)\n   - Reproducibility (1-5)\n   - Overall Assessment (1-5, half-point scale per EMNLP 2026)\n   - Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Calibrate to EMNLP acceptance rate (~21% main, plus Findings). A paper scoring 4.0 is borderline Main acceptance.","uuid":"d4543b2b-4674-4fda-88f5-5dd601a432c4","session_id":"ed42e909-7586-4fce-8151-53367c735e4f"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer R1 for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus is **methods and experiments**. You are doing a blind review — evaluate the paper purely on its merits.\n\n## Venue Calibration (EMNLP 2026 via ARR)\n- 5 = Award (top 2.5%), 4 = Main conference, 3 = Findings, 2 = Resubmit, 1 = Do not resubmit\n- Dimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5)\n- Acceptance rate ~21% for Main\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"All Ambiguity Is Nocuous: A Causal Study of Policy Specification Ambiguity and Agent Safety Violations\"\n\nThe paper studies how linguistic ambiguity in natural-language policy specifications causes safety violations in LLM agents. Using a matched-pair design, 300 clause pairs across 6 ambiguity types, 4 models (GPT-5.4, GPT-4.1, Qwen3-32B-AWQ, Qwen3-8B), and 2,400 agentic episodes on τ²-bench, they establish 5 claims:\n- C1: Ambiguous clauses cause +33.6 pp increase in violation rate (all 6 types p < 3e-7)\n- C2: Ambiguity type does not differentially predict violation severity (χ²(5)=5.20, p=0.39, V=0.066) — binary detection suffices\n- C3: Visible conflicts (CP) yield fewer violations than invisible ambiguity (scopal): Δ=-11pp, p=0.015\n- C4: Model capability predicts type sensitivity — stronger models show wider type-specific ranges (22pp vs 4pp), interaction p=0.018\n- C5: Error analysis identifies 8 failure modes with strong type-clustering\n\nKey tables: Table 1 (binary effect), Table 2 (type uniformity), Table 3 (visibility), Table 4 (4×6 violation matrix), Table 5 (failure mode taxonomy), Table 6 (visibility mechanism evidence), Table 7 (classifier PoC).\n\nAppendices cover: taxonomy cross-walk (A), clause generation (B), reliability ICC=0.82 (C), realism validation with KS tests all non-significant (D).\n\nPaper is 8 pages main text + references + appendices. Anonymous submission.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full experimental plan included these experiments:\n1. full_study_clause_generation: 300 clauses (50/type × 6 types), Chantree-style quality audit, 94% pass rate\n2. full_study_api: 1,200 episodes with GPT-5.4 and GPT-4.1\n3. full_study_local: 1,200 episodes with Qwen3-32B-AWQ and Qwen3-8B via vLLM\n4. full_study_analysis: Statistical analysis — binary effect, omnibus χ², visibility, interaction\n5. error_analysis: 300 violation traces, 8 failure modes, inter-annotator κ=0.78\n6. reliability_test: 30 clauses × 3 replicates × 4 models = 720 episodes, ICC=0.82\n7. realism_validation: KS tests comparing synthetic vs DRIP-R natural ambiguities\n8. classifier_poc: DeBERTa-v3-large binary classifier, 88% accuracy, AUC 0.93\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, specific)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, specific, with severity: major/minor)\n4. **Methodological Scrutiny** — evaluate:\n   - Matched-pair design validity and confounds\n   - Statistical analysis: appropriate tests? Multiple comparison corrections? TOST equivalence logic?\n   - Cross-judge evaluation (κ=0.91): is this sufficient? Any circularity risk with LLM judge?\n   - Reliability study: ICC interpretation, sample size for reliability\n   - Realism validation: KS tests + discriminator — sufficient for ecological validity?\n   - Classifier PoC: role in paper, appropriate scope?\n5. **Experimental Coverage** — compare what the paper reports vs. the experiment list above. Are key results missing or underreported?\n6. **Questions for Authors** (3-5 specific questions)\n7. **Scores**:\n   - Soundness (1-5)\n   - Excitement (1-5)\n   - Reproducibility (1-5)\n   - Overall Assessment (1-5, half-point scale per EMNLP 2026)\n   - Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Calibrate to EMNLP acceptance rate (~21% main, plus Findings). A paper scoring 4.0 is borderline Main acceptance."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01WjqTeLLLBSPS4fQbQ6HiJR","session_id":"ed42e909-7586-4fce-8151-53367c735e4f","uuid":"c21be42e-9755-4e39-8c5b-b114d6e1af85","timestamp":"2026-05-20T12:06:04.234Z"}
