{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01HzJKa3HTZp9Wmvx3gyHa82","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012gYiJwbmYuUgfWm3PApy4q","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0519-4/projects/policy_ambiguity_safety/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T112859/best/paper.tex","limit":80},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19176,"cache_read_input_tokens":40556,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":19176,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01TcYtz7RYNnCem9Qyo7Wigv","session_id":"ed42e909-7586-4fce-8151-53367c735e4f","uuid":"f7bd8e6f-fec9-488a-8fa9-c332e3c47b97"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012gYiJwbmYuUgfWm3PApy4q","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n6\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n7\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n8\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n11\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n12\t\\usepackage{array}\n13\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n14\t\n15\t\\newcommand{\\pp}{\\,\\text{pp}}\n16\t\\newcommand{\\ie}{\\textit{i.e.}}\n17\t\\newcommand{\\eg}{\\textit{e.g.}}\n18\t\n19\t\\title{All Ambiguity Is Nocuous: A Causal Study of Policy Specification Ambiguity and Agent Safety Violations}\n20\t\n21\t\\author{Anonymous}\n22\t\n23\t\\begin{document}\n24\t\\maketitle\n25\t\n26\t\\begin{abstract}\n27\tLLM agents increasingly operate under natural-language policy specifications, yet the safety impact of linguistic ambiguity in these policies remains unquantified.\n28\tWe present the first causal, type-granularity study of how policy ambiguity drives agent safety violations.\n29\tUsing a matched-pair design across 6 linguistically grounded ambiguity types, 4 models (2 frontier API, 2 open-source), and 2,400 agentic episodes on the $\\tau^2$-bench framework, we establish five findings:\n30\t(1)~ambiguous policy clauses cause a $+33.6\\pp$ increase in safety violation rates (all 6 types individually significant, McNemar $p < 3 \\times 10^{-7}$);\n31\t(2)~ambiguity type does not differentially predict violation severity (omnibus $\\chi^2(5)=5.20$, $p=0.39$, Cram\\'er's $V=0.066$), implying binary detection suffices for safety triage;\n32\t(3)~explicitly visible rule conflicts yield \\emph{fewer} violations than structurally invisible ambiguity ($\\Delta=-11\\pp$, $p=0.015$), revealing that ambiguity \\emph{visibility}, not severity, moderates risk;\n33\t(4)~model capability predicts type sensitivity---stronger models show wider type-specific violation ranges (22$\\pp$ vs.\\ 4$\\pp$), with a significant model$\\times$type interaction ($p=0.018$);\n34\t(5)~qualitative error analysis identifies 8 failure-mode categories with strong type-clustering, providing a mechanistic account of \\emph{how} different ambiguities cause distinct failures even when \\emph{how much} they cause is equivalent.\n35\tConnecting Berry \\& Kamsties' nocuous ambiguity theory to agent safety, we argue that all policy ambiguity is nocuous for LLM agents---unlike human requirements engineering where shared context renders most ambiguity innocuous.\n36\tA proof-of-concept binary ambiguity classifier achieves 88\\% accuracy (AUC 0.93), demonstrating the practical viability of our findings.\n37\t\\end{abstract}\n38\t\n39\t%==============================================================================\n40\t\\section{Introduction}\n41\t%==============================================================================\n42\t\n43\tLLM-based agents are increasingly deployed to execute tasks governed by natural-language policy specifications: customer service scripts, compliance rules, authorization protocols.\n44\tUnlike executable code, these policies inherit all the ambiguity inherent to natural language.\n45\tPrior work has catalogued ambiguity types in policy-like documents \\cite{li2024ambiguity,massey2014requirements,berry2003ambiguity}, and concurrent work measures how ambiguity degrades agent \\emph{decision quality} \\cite{dripr2026}.\n46\tHowever, no study has causally quantified how policy ambiguity translates into agent \\emph{safety violations}---actions that breach the intended constraints of a policy.\n47\t\n48\tThis gap matters because safety and decision quality are distinct constructs.\n49\tAn agent may make a suboptimal decision without violating safety constraints, or may violate a constraint while producing an otherwise reasonable outcome.\n50\tFurthermore, the relationship between ambiguity \\emph{type} and safety risk is unknown: should practitioners invest in type-specific detection and resolution pipelines, or does binary ambiguity detection suffice?\n51\t\n52\tBerry and Kamsties~\\cite{berry2003ambiguity} distinguish \\emph{nocuous} ambiguity (leading to divergent interpretations and errors) from \\emph{innocuous} ambiguity (where shared organizational context converges interpretations).\n53\tIn human requirements engineering, most ambiguity is innocuous because stakeholders share tacit domain knowledge.\n54\tLLM agents, however, lack this shared context---raising the question: \\emph{Is all policy ambiguity nocuous for agents?}\n55\t\n56\tWe present the first causal, type-granularity study of policy ambiguity and agent safety.\n57\tOur matched-pair design isolates the causal effect of ambiguity by comparing each ambiguous clause with a minimally edited unambiguous variant, controlling for content, length, and domain.\n58\tWe test 300 clause pairs across 6 linguistically grounded ambiguity types, 4 models spanning frontier API and open-source systems, and 2 policy domains using the $\\tau^2$-bench agentic evaluation framework.\n59\t\n60\tOur contributions are:\n61\t\\begin{enumerate}\n62\t    \\item \\textbf{Empirical}: The first causal map from ambiguity type to safety violation rate, quantifying effect sizes ($+33.6\\pp$ aggregate, all types $p<3\\times10^{-7}$) across 2,400 agentic episodes.\n63\t    \\item \\textbf{Theoretical}: All policy ambiguity is nocuous for LLM agents. Unlike human RE, no type produces innocuous outcomes. Ambiguity \\emph{visibility} moderates risk ($\\Delta=-11\\pp$, $p=0.015$).\n64\t    \\item \\textbf{Practical}: Binary ambiguity detection suffices for safety triage---type-specific pipelines are not justified by safety data ($V=0.066$). A proof-of-concept classifier achieves 88\\% accuracy.\n65\t    \\item \\textbf{Methodological}: A matched-pair causal design with cross-judge evaluation and a 6-type taxonomy cross-walked to three prior frameworks.\n66\t\\end{enumerate}\n67\t\n68\t%==============================================================================\n69\t\\section{Related Work}\n70\t%==============================================================================\n71\t\n72\t\\paragraph{Ambiguity in NLP and Requirements Engineering.}\n73\tLinguistic ambiguity has been extensively taxonomized in both NLP~\\cite{wasow2005intuitions,kennedy2007vagueness} and requirements engineering~\\cite{berry2003ambiguity,massey2014requirements}.\n74\tLi et al.~\\cite{li2024ambiguity} propose an 11-type taxonomy for NLP policy documents; Massey et al.~\\cite{massey2014requirements} identify 6 types in privacy policies.\n75\tBerry and Kamsties~\\cite{berry2003ambiguity} introduce the nocuous/innocuous distinction based on whether ambiguity causes divergent interpretations.\n76\tChantree et al.~\\cite{chantree2006identifying} develop automated ambiguity identification in requirements.\n77\tOur taxonomy draws on all four works, selecting 6 types that are both frequent in policy documents and theoretically distinct.\n78\t\n79\t\\paragraph{Agent Safety Evaluation.}\n80\tRecent benchmarks evaluate agent safety along various axes: $\\tau^2$-bench~\\cite{tau2bench} measures policy compliance in agentic customer-service episodes; PolicyBank and PhantomPolicy study constraint adherence under adversarial conditions."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01TcYtz7RYNnCem9Qyo7Wigv","session_id":"ed42e909-7586-4fce-8151-53367c735e4f","uuid":"a173c03c-16f6-485c-ade8-9e9efbe7441c","timestamp":"2026-05-20T12:10:20.874Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"aedd36dfb32468746","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y9LMtgBZ5bEnCMfQ5RviEh","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R2 reacts to R1's review","usage":{"total_tokens":47947,"tool_uses":13,"duration_ms":74772},"uuid":"48baf9f4-5974-455d-b726-81ba0c0f0218","session_id":"ed42e909-7586-4fce-8151-53367c735e4f"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y9LMtgBZ5bEnCMfQ5RviEh","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the paper, the \"best\" scenario experimental plan, and the actual median paper. Let me construct my R2 response to R1's review.\n\nKey observations:\n- The \"best\" scenario had C3 at p=0.015 and C4 at p=0.023, but the actual median paper has C3 at p=0.09 and C4 at p=0.14\n- The median paper reports κ=0.78 for cross-judge, not 0.91\n- The paper lacks the plan_009 judge calibration study\n- The paper has no figures at all\n- The median paper has C2 omnibus p=0.17 (not 0.39 or 0.50)\n\nNow I need to reconcile R1's review (which references some \"best\" numbers like p=0.39 for C2, p=0.015 for C3, κ=0.91) with the median paper's actual numbers. R1 seems to have reviewed a version mixing best and median numbers. Let me re-read the paper carefully to note the exact values.\n\nLooking at the paper:\n- C2: χ²(5)=7.82, p=0.17, V=0.15 (paper says this)\n- C3: p=0.09, Cohen's h=0.20, Δ=-7.2pp (paper says this)\n- Cross-judge κ=0.78 (paper says this)\n- Error analysis κ=0.52 (paper says this)\n\nR1's review cites some different numbers (p=0.39 for C2, p=0.015 for C3, κ=0.91). This suggests R1 may have reviewed the \"best\" scenario version. But the task says to respond to R1's review as-is, so I'll engage with their points on their merits.\n\nLet me now write my response.\n\n---\n\n**R2 Response to R1's Review**\n\n**1. Points of agreement:**\n\nR1's M2 (missing judge calibration) is the strongest point in either review. I flagged LLM-as-judge concerns in my own review but did not notice the plan_009 gap. R1 is correct that the experiment plan included 100 human-annotated episodes for judge validation, and these results are absent from the paper. Without this calibration, the entire measurement chain -- from episode to violation label -- is unvalidated against human ground truth. I now consider this the single most critical deficiency. I also agree with M5 (missing figures): an 8-page empirical paper with zero visualizations is below EMNLP presentation standards, and the experiment plan described 6+ figures that simply did not materialize.\n\nI agree with the spirit of M4 (incompleteness information asymmetry). The paper acknowledges this in Limitations but does not quantify the information delta between matched pairs, leaving the confound unresolved.\n\n**2. Points of disagreement or overstatement:**\n\nM1 (C2/C3 tension) is overstated. The paper I reviewed reports C3 at p=0.09 (not 0.015), explicitly framed as a \"suggestive\" trend rather than a confirmed claim. There is no logical contradiction between a non-significant omnibus test over 6 types and a directional trend in one pairwise contrast -- the former tests whether the full type vector predicts violations, the latter tests a specific theoretical hypothesis about visibility. These address different questions. If C3 had been reported as p=0.015 and promoted to a core claim, M1 would be more damaging; at p=0.09 with appropriate hedging, the tension is managed.\n\nM3 (multiple comparison correction for C3) is technically valid but somewhat mechanical. The visibility hypothesis has a clear theoretical motivation from psycholinguistic garden-path literature, and the paper does not claim significance. Treating a pre-motivated directional hypothesis identically to 14 other atheoretical pairwise comparisons is overly conservative.\n\nR1's concern about conservative refusal coding (m7) is interesting but minor. Table 5 codes \"conservative refusal then proceed\" as a violation failure mode (18%), which is internally consistent: the agent ultimately acts, causing a violation. The naming is slightly misleading but the coding logic is sound.\n\n**3. Score revision:**\n\nR1's M2 (missing judge calibration) lowers my confidence in the evaluation pipeline more than my original review accounted for. I revise my Overall from 3.0 to 2.5 (Borderline Findings). Without human calibration data, the +31pp effect could partially reflect judge bias rather than genuine violations. The missing figures compound the incompleteness impression.\n\n**4. Most important unresolved issue:**\n\nThe judge calibration gap (M2). R1 and I agree on its importance but differ on whether it is fatal versus fixable. I believe the authors could address this in a revision cycle by reporting the plan_009 results, but its current absence undermines the paper's central empirical claim. This should be the primary discussion point if an author response is solicited."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: aedd36dfb32468746 (use SendMessage with to: 'aedd36dfb32468746' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 48590\ntool_uses: 13\nduration_ms: 74773</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ed42e909-7586-4fce-8151-53367c735e4f","uuid":"5fc54fbe-b3c8-4e0b-9ac1-69cbf2a8924e","timestamp":"2026-05-20T12:10:47.025Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 (novelty + related work focus). You previously reviewed \"All Ambiguity Is Nocuous\" and gave it Overall 3.0 (Borderline Findings).\n\nReviewer R1 (methods + experiments focus) has submitted their independent review. Here is their review:\n\n---\n**R1 Scores:** Soundness 3.5, Excitement 3.5, Reproducibility 3.0, Overall 3.5 (Borderline Conference), Confidence 4\n\n**R1 Key Weaknesses:**\n1. (Major, M1) C2/C3 tension — C2 says type doesn't predict severity (omnibus p=0.39), but C3 says CP vs scopal differ (p=0.015). Unresolved.\n2. (Major, M2) LLM-as-judge circularity — κ=0.91 measures LLM-LLM agreement, not accuracy. The experiment plan included judge calibration with 100 human-annotated episodes (plan_009) but results are ABSENT from the paper. This is the single most important missing validation.\n3. (Major, M3) C3 may not survive multiple comparison correction — if treated as one of 15 pairwise contrasts, p=0.015 × 15 = 0.225. Was it pre-registered or post-hoc?\n4. (Major, M4) Incompleteness information asymmetry — unambiguous variant adds information, inflating the effect.\n5. (Minor, m3) Missing figures — no visualizations in 8-page paper despite experiment plan describing 6+ figures.\n6. (Minor, m5) No Claude models.\n7. (Minor, m6) Conservative refusal coded in failure taxonomy despite being described as \"non-violation.\"\n\n**R1 Key Questions:**\n- C2/C3 reconciliation\n- Judge calibration results (plan_009)\n- TOST margin justification\n- Incompleteness information delta measurement\n- Conservative refusal classification inconsistency\n\n**R1 Novel Concern not in your review:**\n- Missing judge calibration (plan_009) — the experiment plan included 100 human-annotated episodes for LLM judge validation. This is absent from the paper. Without it, the entire evaluation chain rests on unvalidated LLM-LLM agreement.\n- C3's pre-registration status — the visibility hypothesis appears to have emerged from diag_001 (post-diagnostic study), suggesting it was exploratory promoted to core claim, not pre-registered.\n- Conservative refusal coding inconsistency in Table 5.\n- Missing figures.\n---\n\n**Your task:** In 300-400 words, respond to R1's review:\n1. Which of R1's points do you agree with and why?\n2. Which do you disagree with or think are overstated?\n3. Does R1's review change your scores? If so, how?\n4. What is the most important unresolved issue between your reviews?","agentId":"aedd36dfb32468746","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the paper, the \"best\" scenario experimental plan, and the actual median paper. Let me construct my R2 response to R1's review.\n\nKey observations:\n- The \"best\" scenario had C3 at p=0.015 and C4 at p=0.023, but the actual median paper has C3 at p=0.09 and C4 at p=0.14\n- The median paper reports κ=0.78 for cross-judge, not 0.91\n- The paper lacks the plan_009 judge calibration study\n- The paper has no figures at all\n- The median paper has C2 omnibus p=0.17 (not 0.39 or 0.50)\n\nNow I need to reconcile R1's review (which references some \"best\" numbers like p=0.39 for C2, p=0.015 for C3, κ=0.91) with the median paper's actual numbers. R1 seems to have reviewed a version mixing best and median numbers. Let me re-read the paper carefully to note the exact values.\n\nLooking at the paper:\n- C2: χ²(5)=7.82, p=0.17, V=0.15 (paper says this)\n- C3: p=0.09, Cohen's h=0.20, Δ=-7.2pp (paper says this)\n- Cross-judge κ=0.78 (paper says this)\n- Error analysis κ=0.52 (paper says this)\n\nR1's review cites some different numbers (p=0.39 for C2, p=0.015 for C3, κ=0.91). This suggests R1 may have reviewed the \"best\" scenario version. But the task says to respond to R1's review as-is, so I'll engage with their points on their merits.\n\nLet me now write my response.\n\n---\n\n**R2 Response to R1's Review**\n\n**1. Points of agreement:**\n\nR1's M2 (missing judge calibration) is the strongest point in either review. I flagged LLM-as-judge concerns in my own review but did not notice the plan_009 gap. R1 is correct that the experiment plan included 100 human-annotated episodes for judge validation, and these results are absent from the paper. Without this calibration, the entire measurement chain -- from episode to violation label -- is unvalidated against human ground truth. I now consider this the single most critical deficiency. I also agree with M5 (missing figures): an 8-page empirical paper with zero visualizations is below EMNLP presentation standards, and the experiment plan described 6+ figures that simply did not materialize.\n\nI agree with the spirit of M4 (incompleteness information asymmetry). The paper acknowledges this in Limitations but does not quantify the information delta between matched pairs, leaving the confound unresolved.\n\n**2. Points of disagreement or overstatement:**\n\nM1 (C2/C3 tension) is overstated. The paper I reviewed reports C3 at p=0.09 (not 0.015), explicitly framed as a \"suggestive\" trend rather than a confirmed claim. There is no logical contradiction between a non-significant omnibus test over 6 types and a directional trend in one pairwise contrast -- the former tests whether the full type vector predicts violations, the latter tests a specific theoretical hypothesis about visibility. These address different questions. If C3 had been reported as p=0.015 and promoted to a core claim, M1 would be more damaging; at p=0.09 with appropriate hedging, the tension is managed.\n\nM3 (multiple comparison correction for C3) is technically valid but somewhat mechanical. The visibility hypothesis has a clear theoretical motivation from psycholinguistic garden-path literature, and the paper does not claim significance. Treating a pre-motivated directional hypothesis identically to 14 other atheoretical pairwise comparisons is overly conservative.\n\nR1's concern about conservative refusal coding (m7) is interesting but minor. Table 5 codes \"conservative refusal then proceed\" as a violation failure mode (18%), which is internally consistent: the agent ultimately acts, causing a violation. The naming is slightly misleading but the coding logic is sound.\n\n**3. Score revision:**\n\nR1's M2 (missing judge calibration) lowers my confidence in the evaluation pipeline more than my original review accounted for. I revise my Overall from 3.0 to 2.5 (Borderline Findings). Without human calibration data, the +31pp effect could partially reflect judge bias rather than genuine violations. The missing figures compound the incompleteness impression.\n\n**4. Most important unresolved issue:**\n\nThe judge calibration gap (M2). R1 and I agree on its importance but differ on whether it is fatal versus fixable. I believe the authors could address this in a revision cycle by reporting the plan_009 results, but its current absence undermines the paper's central empirical claim. This should be the primary discussion point if an author response is solicited."}],"totalDurationMs":74773,"totalTokens":48590,"totalToolUseCount":13,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2904,"cache_read_input_tokens":44504,"output_tokens":1181,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2904},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":1181,"cache_read_input_tokens":44504,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2904,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2904,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":4,"searchCount":6,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":3}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"afa827658b2386fce","tool_use_id":"toolu_01TcYtz7RYNnCem9Qyo7Wigv","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R1 reacts to R2's review","usage":{"total_tokens":62771,"tool_uses":17,"duration_ms":88625},"uuid":"9d25b45a-1103-46f9-9a87-51323b0f5716","session_id":"ed42e909-7586-4fce-8151-53367c735e4f"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TcYtz7RYNnCem9Qyo7Wigv","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have comprehensive understanding of the paper, its claims, data, and framing. Let me now write my R1 meta-review response to R2.\n\n---\n\n**R1 Response to R2's Review**\n\n**Points of Agreement.** R2's prompt sensitivity confound (their novel concern) is the strongest point in either of our reviews. The +33.6pp effect is attributed entirely to ambiguity, but without a control condition using non-ambiguity perturbations (e.g., syntactic complexity increase, clause reordering, or irrelevant information insertion), we cannot cleanly separate ambiguity-specific harm from general prompt brittleness. I flagged the ecological validity of synthetic injection in my review, but R2 sharpens this into a more precise methodological gap. The authors' matched-pair design controls content but not perturbation-type. This is actionable: even a small control arm with non-ambiguous edits of similar surface magnitude would substantially strengthen the causal claim.\n\nI also agree on the thin reference list. Eight references for a paper bridging CL, RE, and agent safety is insufficient. The missing prompt sensitivity citations (Sclar et al. 2024) are particularly relevant given the perturbation confound above. The garden-path citations (Frazier & Rayner 1982) are essential if the paper invokes visibility/reanalysis mechanisms for C3.\n\n**Points of Disagreement.** R2's C2/C3 tension concern is overstated. C2 tests the omnibus hypothesis across all 6 types (V=0.066); C3 is a planned pairwise contrast between the two most theoretically distinct types (CP vs. scopal). These answer different questions. The omnibus null says type information has negligible aggregate predictive value; the pairwise significant result says one specific structural distinction (visibility) matters at the extremes. This is not internal tension--it is a standard coarse-vs-fine analysis pattern. The 10.7pp range being \"negligible\" for omnibus but the 11pp CP-scopal delta being \"significant\" reflects that the middle four types cluster tightly (40-42%), so variability is concentrated in the tails. R2 conflates the TOST equivalence margin with the pairwise test, which have different statistical targets.\n\nR2's Berry & Kamsties operationalization mismatch point has merit in principle but is acknowledged in the paper's framing: the authors explicitly redefine nocuity for the agent setting (agent-vs-intent deviation) rather than claiming to test inter-reader disagreement. This is a deliberate theoretical extension, not an oversight.\n\n**Score Revision.** R2's prompt sensitivity confound gives me pause. If the +33.6pp partly reflects general perturbation sensitivity, the title claim weakens. However, the error analysis (C5) showing type-specific failure modes (scope misapplication, referent misidentification, etc.) provides strong qualitative evidence that the violations are ambiguity-mechanism-specific, not generic noise. I maintain my Overall 3.5 but would revise Soundness from 3.5 to 3.0 if the authors cannot address the perturbation control in revision.\n\n**Most Important Unresolved Issue.** The perturbation confound. Both reviewers should insist on a non-ambiguity perturbation control arm (even small, e.g., 50 clauses) before the causal claim in the title can be fully sustained. This is the single revision that would most strengthen the paper."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: afa827658b2386fce (use SendMessage with to: 'afa827658b2386fce' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 62755\ntool_uses: 17\nduration_ms: 88627</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ed42e909-7586-4fce-8151-53367c735e4f","uuid":"f81932ee-a98c-4f4a-a632-a10511a8683d","timestamp":"2026-05-20T12:10:47.520Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 (methods + experiments focus). You previously reviewed \"All Ambiguity Is Nocuous\" and gave it Overall 3.5 (Borderline Conference).\n\nReviewer R2 (novelty + related work focus) has submitted their independent review. Here is their review:\n\n---\n**R2 Scores:** Soundness 3.5, Excitement 3.5, Reproducibility 3.0, Overall 3.0 (Borderline Findings), Confidence 4\n\n**R2 Key Weaknesses:**\n1. (Major) Title claim exceeds evidence — \"All Ambiguity Is Nocuous\" is universal claim tested on 6 of 11+ types, 2 domains, synthetic injections. Untested types exist.\n2. (Major) C2 null over-interpretation — TOST margin ±15pp is generous. 10.7pp CP-scopal range is simultaneously used as evidence for C3 (significant) while argued negligible for C2. Internal tension.\n3. (Major) Ecological validity — single injected clause may behave differently from naturally ambiguous document.\n4. (Minor) No Claude model tested.\n5. (Minor) Thin PoC classifier — no baselines (e.g., zero-shot GPT detection).\n6. (Minor) Reference list is thin — only 8 references. Missing: Willis et al. 2008, Yang et al. 2011, Ezzini et al. 2022, prompt sensitivity literature (Sclar et al. 2024), garden-path citations (Frazier & Rayner 1982), agent safety benchmarks (AgentPex, ShieldAgent).\n7. (Minor) Two-domain limitation.\n\n**R2 Key Questions:**\n- C2/C3 tension: how reconciled?\n- Inter-model disagreement: did they test whether different models adopt different readings (true Berry & Kamsties nocuity)?\n- Prompt sensitivity confound: could random perturbations show similar violation increases?\n- Classifier baselines: zero-shot GPT performance?\n\n**R2 Novel Concern not in your review:**\n- Prompt sensitivity confound: the +33.6pp effect might partly reflect general prompt brittleness rather than ambiguity-specific failure. Without a control condition (non-ambiguity perturbation), this cannot be distinguished.\n- Berry & Kamsties operationalization mismatch: the paper tests agent-vs-intent deviation, not inter-reader disagreement.\n---\n\n**Your task:** In 300-400 words, respond to R2's review:\n1. Which of R2's points do you agree with and why?\n2. Which do you disagree with or think are overstated?\n3. Does R2's review change your scores? If so, how?\n4. What is the most important unresolved issue between your reviews?","agentId":"afa827658b2386fce","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have comprehensive understanding of the paper, its claims, data, and framing. Let me now write my R1 meta-review response to R2.\n\n---\n\n**R1 Response to R2's Review**\n\n**Points of Agreement.** R2's prompt sensitivity confound (their novel concern) is the strongest point in either of our reviews. The +33.6pp effect is attributed entirely to ambiguity, but without a control condition using non-ambiguity perturbations (e.g., syntactic complexity increase, clause reordering, or irrelevant information insertion), we cannot cleanly separate ambiguity-specific harm from general prompt brittleness. I flagged the ecological validity of synthetic injection in my review, but R2 sharpens this into a more precise methodological gap. The authors' matched-pair design controls content but not perturbation-type. This is actionable: even a small control arm with non-ambiguous edits of similar surface magnitude would substantially strengthen the causal claim.\n\nI also agree on the thin reference list. Eight references for a paper bridging CL, RE, and agent safety is insufficient. The missing prompt sensitivity citations (Sclar et al. 2024) are particularly relevant given the perturbation confound above. The garden-path citations (Frazier & Rayner 1982) are essential if the paper invokes visibility/reanalysis mechanisms for C3.\n\n**Points of Disagreement.** R2's C2/C3 tension concern is overstated. C2 tests the omnibus hypothesis across all 6 types (V=0.066); C3 is a planned pairwise contrast between the two most theoretically distinct types (CP vs. scopal). These answer different questions. The omnibus null says type information has negligible aggregate predictive value; the pairwise significant result says one specific structural distinction (visibility) matters at the extremes. This is not internal tension--it is a standard coarse-vs-fine analysis pattern. The 10.7pp range being \"negligible\" for omnibus but the 11pp CP-scopal delta being \"significant\" reflects that the middle four types cluster tightly (40-42%), so variability is concentrated in the tails. R2 conflates the TOST equivalence margin with the pairwise test, which have different statistical targets.\n\nR2's Berry & Kamsties operationalization mismatch point has merit in principle but is acknowledged in the paper's framing: the authors explicitly redefine nocuity for the agent setting (agent-vs-intent deviation) rather than claiming to test inter-reader disagreement. This is a deliberate theoretical extension, not an oversight.\n\n**Score Revision.** R2's prompt sensitivity confound gives me pause. If the +33.6pp partly reflects general perturbation sensitivity, the title claim weakens. However, the error analysis (C5) showing type-specific failure modes (scope misapplication, referent misidentification, etc.) provides strong qualitative evidence that the violations are ambiguity-mechanism-specific, not generic noise. I maintain my Overall 3.5 but would revise Soundness from 3.5 to 3.0 if the authors cannot address the perturbation control in revision.\n\n**Most Important Unresolved Issue.** The perturbation confound. Both reviewers should insist on a non-ambiguity perturbation control arm (even small, e.g., 50 clauses) before the causal claim in the title can be fully sustained. This is the single revision that would most strengthen the paper."}],"totalDurationMs":88627,"totalTokens":62755,"totalToolUseCount":17,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2219,"cache_read_input_tokens":59732,"output_tokens":803,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":